course: dictionary
lesson: 10620
title: Unfairness and what it means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unfairness is a noun. It means the quality of not being fair, or one example of being unfair. Look at the words around it to work out the meaning: «Feeling angry about unfairness is natural, and that anger can lead people to help others or change things.»
explain: Angry, help and change are the clues. Unfairness is something that makes people feel angry and want to put things right. In «to stand up to unfairness and say «Nonsense!»», the words stand up to show that unfairness is something you can push back against.
explain: Unfairness often shows up in games, rules and sharing. If one person gets much more than another, the parts are not equal. To check your meaning, swap in "not being fair" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Unfairness means the quality of not being fair.
fact: Unfairness can also mean one example of being unfair.
fact: Fair means equal and just, so unfair means not equal and not just.
fact: In sharing, unfairness means the parts are not equal.
fact: Unfairness can make people angry, and that anger can lead them to help or change things.
exercise: A girl in a book would «stand up to unfairness and say «Nonsense!»». What does unfairness mean here? | A happy surprise at tea-time | Being very tired after a long day | *Treating people in an unjust way | Losing a game on purpose
exercise: «Feeling angry about unfairness is natural.» What feeling does unfairness usually cause? | Surprise | Sleepiness | *Anger | Coldness
exercise: Two children share 10 sweets. One gets 8 and the other gets 2. What is the unfairness here? | *The parts are not equal | Both children are very hungry | The parts are exactly the same | There are no sweets left
test: A player shouts, «That's unfair!» What does the player mean? | The player has won the game | The game is very fun | The player is tired and wants a drink | *The rule is not equal and not just
test: Which word is the opposite of unfairness? | *Fairness | Kindness | Rudeness | Loudness
exam: The teacher gave the biggest slice of cake to one child and the smallest to another, with no reason. What is this an example of? | A way to bake a cake | A kind surprise | A type of weather | *Unfairness
